# External SMTP submission per Identity

External SMTP submission lets each user send outbound mail through an external
provider -- Gmail, Microsoft 365, Fastmail, or a corporate relay -- while
herold handles storage, search, and the suite UI. The configuration is
per-Identity: a user with multiple Identities can route some through herold's
outbound queue and others through external providers.

Inbound mail is independent of external SMTP submission. Herold has an IMAP
client mode that fetches mail from external providers, configured separately
from outbound submission. Forwarding at the provider is also an option. Configure it using the
provider's own mechanism (Gmail "Forwarding and POP/IMAP", the Microsoft 365
mailbox forwarding rule, or equivalent); mail forwarded to herold's receiving
address arrives through the normal inbound pipeline.

## Prerequisite: secrets data key

When a user configures external SMTP submission, they enter their credentials
for the external provider (for example, a Gmail app password or an OAuth token)
into herold so herold can send mail on their behalf. Herold encrypts those
credentials at rest using a data key before writing them to the database.

Generate a key:

```
openssl rand -hex 64 | head -c 64
```

Store the key material where herold's operating user can read it at startup,
then set `[server.secrets].data_key_ref` in `system.toml` to point to it.
Two reference forms are accepted:

**Environment variable:**

```toml
[server.secrets]
data_key_ref = "$HEROLD_DATA_KEY"
```

Set `HEROLD_DATA_KEY` to the 64-hex string in the process environment. With
systemd, use `EnvironmentFile=` or `Environment=`. With Docker or Kubernetes,
mount a Secret as an environment variable.

**File:**

```toml
[server.secrets]
data_key_ref = "file:/var/lib/herold/data_key.hex"
```

Write the 64-hex string to the file and restrict access:

```
install -m 0600 -o herold /dev/stdin /var/lib/herold/data_key.hex <<'EOF'
<paste 64-hex string here>
EOF
```

Inline values in `system.toml` are rejected at startup to prevent accidental
commits of secret material.

## Enable external submission

Add to `/etc/herold/system.toml`:

```toml
[server.external_submission]
enabled = true
```

Then reload:

```
herold server reload
```

If `[server.secrets].data_key_ref` is absent or resolves to fewer than
32 bytes, the server refuses to start:

```
sysconfig: [server.external_submission] enabled requires [server.secrets].data_key_ref; set [server.secrets].data_key_ref to enable [server.external_submission]
```

## OAuth providers (optional)

Registering an OAuth provider lets users send on behalf of their Google or
Microsoft account without creating application passwords -- they authorize via
OAuth instead. The one-click "Sign in with Google" or "Sign in with Microsoft"
flow appears in the suite's Identity settings when a matching provider is
configured. Without a registered provider, users fall back to manual SMTP
credential entry.

The redirect URI is the same for all Identities on your deployment:

```
https://<your-herold-hostname>/api/v1/oauth/external-submission/callback
```

### Gmail and Google Workspace

1. Open [console.cloud.google.com](https://console.cloud.google.com) and create or select a project.

2. Enable the Gmail API under "APIs & Services" > "Library".

3. Configure the OAuth consent screen under "APIs & Services" > "OAuth consent
   screen". For Google Workspace deployments set the publishing status to
   "Internal". For personal Gmail accounts set it to "External" (Google app
   verification is required for more than 100 users). Add the scope
   `https://mail.google.com/`.

4. Create an OAuth client ID under "APIs & Services" > "Credentials" >
   "Create Credentials" > "OAuth client ID". Set the application type to
   "Web application". Add the redirect URI above.

5. Store the client secret in an environment variable or file.

6. Add to `/etc/herold/system.toml`:

   ```toml
   [server.oauth_providers.gmail]
   client_id         = "123456789012-abc.apps.googleusercontent.com"
   client_secret_ref = "$HEROLD_OAUTH_GMAIL_SECRET"
   auth_url          = "https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth"
   token_url         = "https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token"
   scopes            = ["https://mail.google.com/"]
   ```

7. Reload: `herold server reload`

When the OAuth flow completes, herold connects to `smtp.gmail.com` port 465
(implicit TLS) automatically.

### Microsoft 365

1. Open `portal.azure.com` and navigate to "Azure Active Directory" (Entra ID)
   > "App registrations" > "New registration". Under "Supported account types"
   choose the option that matches your tenant scope.

2. Under "Redirect URI", set the platform to "Web" and enter the redirect URI
   above.

3. Under "API permissions" > "Add a permission" > "APIs my organization uses" >
   search for "Office 365 Exchange Online". Select "Delegated permissions" and
   add `SMTP.Send`. Grant admin consent if your tenant requires it.

4. Under "Certificates & secrets" > "Client secrets" > "New client secret".
   Copy the value immediately.

5. Store the client secret in an environment variable or file.

6. Add to `/etc/herold/system.toml`. The `common` tenant endpoint works for
   both personal Microsoft accounts and work/school accounts; replace `common`
   with your tenant ID to restrict to a single tenant:

   ```toml
   [server.oauth_providers.m365]
   client_id         = "<application-client-id-from-azure>"
   client_secret_ref = "$HEROLD_OAUTH_M365_SECRET"
   auth_url          = "https://login.microsoftonline.com/common/oauth2/v2.0/authorize"
   token_url         = "https://login.microsoftonline.com/common/oauth2/v2.0/token"
   scopes            = ["https://outlook.office365.com/SMTP.Send", "offline_access"]
   ```

   `offline_access` is required. Without it, Microsoft's token endpoint does
   not issue a refresh token, and users must re-authenticate each time the
   access token expires.

7. Reload: `herold server reload`

When the OAuth flow completes, herold connects to `smtp.office365.com` port 465
(implicit TLS) automatically.

## Manual credentials

When no OAuth provider is configured for an Identity's email domain, or when
the user prefers not to use OAuth, the Settings dialog accepts manual SMTP
credentials. Before saving, herold runs a live probe to verify the credentials;
if the probe fails, the error is shown inline and nothing is saved.

Users enter the settings themselves under Settings > Identities. Common
configurations:

| Provider | Host | Port | Security | Notes |
|----------|------|------|----------|-------|
| Gmail | smtp.gmail.com | 465 | implicit TLS | Requires an app password (Google Account > Security > 2-Step Verification > App passwords) |
| Microsoft 365 | smtp.office365.com | 587 | STARTTLS | Legacy auth must be enabled at the tenant level and on the mailbox |
| Fastmail | smtp.fastmail.com | 465 | implicit TLS | Use an app password from Fastmail's "Password & Security" settings |

Also ensure the external provider accepts the chosen `From:` address: Gmail
requires "Send mail as" verification in Gmail settings; Microsoft 365 requires
send-as permissions on the mailbox or an Exchange Online shared mailbox.

## Troubleshooting

The suite shows a warning badge on any Identity whose external submission is
in a failed state. Users can click the badge to open the Identity settings.

**auth-failed** -- the last authentication attempt was rejected by the remote
server. For password credentials: the app password was revoked or expired; ask
the user to generate a new one and re-enter it in Settings. For OAuth: the
authorization was revoked at the provider; ask the user to click the
"Re-authenticate" button on the warning badge or in the compose toast. If many
Identities across different users enter auth-failed simultaneously, the
operator-level OAuth client secret may have been rotated at the provider
without updating `system.toml`; update `client_secret_ref` and reload.

**unreachable** -- herold cannot reach the submission host. Verify outbound
TCP connectivity from the herold host:

```
nc -zv smtp.gmail.com 465
```

Cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure, Hetzner Cloud) commonly block outbound TCP
on ports 465 and 587 by default; request an egress allowance from the
provider's networking console or security group settings.

For deeper diagnostics -- metrics, structured-log fields, sweeper behaviour,
and the procedure for handling credential material in logs -- consult the
external-submission on-call runbook kept with the herold server documentation.
